#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116181} :activity/id #uuid "1951cec3-dd4e-5fa8-b2fb-4a6357c4d878" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048539 :topic/id #uuid "a8385b0e-c407-5162-b441-31d369d21310" :topic/math-academy-id 1677} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116181 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116407 :tutorial/content "Consider the region bounded by the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nthe horizontal line $y=1$, and the vertical line $x=3$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/99/990769dd023de72d2cc4caf3e71d161f6af0605962eb20c8a0d71e67a6ff9b95.png)\n\nLet's rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the line $y=1$ to get the solid shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/e1/e12187d889e7389c4d05e5e91245f555aab4e1c2395ef05168cdf0e3f13b6fe3.png)\n\nWe can find the volume using the disc method:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\left[ r(x) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nHere, $r(x)$ is the radius of the disc that corresponds to each point $x$ under consideration.\n\n**Watch out!** Since the axis of revolution is $y=1$ (not $y=0$), we need to account for the change in the corresponding discs' radius.\n\nThe radius is *not* just $\\sqrt{x}$. Rather, the radius is the distance between the line $y=1$ and the function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d33fec84ada995fe3593132ae1567c591c030ed458e5cd923180bc2204f4fb4e.png)\n\nTo find an expression for the radius, we can take the difference between the upper function and the lower function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(x) &= (upper function) - (lower function) \\\\\n&= (\\sqrt{x}) - (1) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{x} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express the volume as the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{1}^{3}(\\sqrt{x} - 1)^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "106b0cc0-d7c1-514d-9829-5d0f6a96ceec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1054 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f729591f-d2a6-5eb4-8ba8-a24bbf4fce16" :step/math-academy-id 2072 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116212}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116182 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115121 :question/id #uuid "7d530adb-8bcc-53f1-b1bf-674be8f7046e" :question/math-academy-id "e-589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c1cc6b6-5253-513c-98b0-9a963895eab6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115964}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"} :step/id #uuid "b5dc1ac9-3a4a-559e-a015-f667b907cb95" :step/math-academy-id 2073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116183}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116183 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116408 :tutorial/content "Consider the region bounded by the curve $y=x^2$, the vertical line $x=1$, and the horizontal line $y=4$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/67/67b5f2a387c6892846ed063934aa3de66cab70410b00741cb3b3c32561a0f9e7.png)\n\nLet's rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the line $x=1$ to get the solid shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f367888095347c2da4173ec97e91df7057c9149d6eab0d7f1a2b62f9180c3a5.png)\n\nTo find the volume of this solid, we can use the disc method, as usual:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{c}^{d} \\left[ r(y) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that the radius $r(y)$ is now horizontal rather than vertical.\n\n![](images/3c/3c9a33c1e3a55e900185002580319af5015cb5b2e0f876228910a98ab42399fa.png)\n\nTo find an expression for the radius, we compute the difference between the right and left functions.\n\n- The right function corresponds to the curve $y=x^2$. Solving for $x$, we get $x = \\pm \\sqrt y$. The region of interest is in the first quadrant, where $x$ is positive, so we must have $x = \\sqrt{y}$.\n- The left function corresponds to the vertical line $x=1$.\n\nSo, the radius is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(y) &= (right function) - (left function) \\\\\n&= (\\sqrt{y}) - (1) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{y} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is represented by the integral\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_1^4 \\left(\\sqrt{y} - 1 \\right)^2 \\,\\textrm{d}y \\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ba1ac8de-1c2e-5016-b0c3-d2384c95f085" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1056 :tutorial/title "Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "0ea52c5b-ebbc-5c8e-b235-3bf8b990211a" :step/math-academy-id 2077 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116184}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116184 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115122 :question/id #uuid "a02ed650-9bbd-552b-9d80-c2d8f1d581b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42ad968e-05d7-5eb6-92aa-c6d4221319a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050548}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115894}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "14bad948-f8d0-5db4-b87e-695426b4530a" :step/math-academy-id 2078 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116213}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116212 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115301 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115126 :question/id #uuid "413e2100-cec8-5c20-981a-73b9a61ddbc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d402001-2d71-5813-9732-fcc950a7ae1b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049042}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115892}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"} :step/id #uuid "c8bfd407-2357-546f-905e-ea3d7ff5c037" :step/math-academy-id 26335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116182}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116213 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115302 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115127 :question/id #uuid "4da6aa8a-844d-53c4-b573-2f81acb5a5c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f948be97-572c-5e4f-a5fa-1291cefbb4ac" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115890}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "69f86211-4281-5a5c-85e2-0f6cdf48203b" :step/math-academy-id 26336}] :activity/title "Volumes of Revolution Using the Disc Method: Rotation About Other Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115453}]}
